index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..b3c9e44f0b2bab47f43e4a778b583315685fb2c5
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/memory/decision-log/dl-001-json-pointer-dedicated-error-type-and-strict-rfc-6901-validation.md
@@ -0,0 +1,34 @@
+---
+id: dl-001-json-pointer-dedicated-error-type-and-strict-rfc-6901-validation
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Pointer: dedicated error type and strict RFC 6901 validation"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+`resolvePointer` must throw on both invalid pointer syntax and pointers that do not resolve to a value
+(RFC 6901 does not mandate how an implementation signals failure). This library is imported by other
+services and is the base for features still to come (e.g. JSON Patch), so its error-handling shape sets
+a precedent.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Throw a single dedicated `JsonPointerError` (exported from `src/index.ts`) for every failure mode,
+  rather than plain `Error`, `TypeError`, or separate classes per failure kind. Callers across the
+  team's services can catch one type to mean "this pointer did not resolve," without the library
+  inventing a taxonomy of failures the RFC itself doesn't define.
+- Validate strictly against RFC 6901 rather than being lenient: reject array indices with leading zeros
+  or the `-` token, reject a `~` not followed by `0` or `1`, and reject a pointer that is non-empty and
+  does not start with `/`. A lenient resolver would silently accept pointers that other RFC-compliant
+  tools (e.g. a future JSON Patch implementation) would reject, producing inconsistent behaviour across
+  the toolkit.
+- Decode escape sequences in the order the RFC specifies — `~1` before `~0` — since reversing the order
+  would corrupt a reference token that legitimately contains the literal text `~1`.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Consumers that want to distinguish "bad input" from "not found" must inspect the error message
+  themselves; the decision can be revisited if a later feature needs that distinction.
+- Future features that build on `resolvePointer` (e.g. JSON Patch) should reuse `JsonPointerError` for
+  their own pointer-related failures rather than introducing a parallel error type.
