--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/memory/task/task-001-implement-json-pointer-resolution-rfc-6901.md
@@ -0,0 +1,34 @@
+---
+id: task-001-implement-json-pointer-resolution-rfc-6901
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Pointer resolution (RFC 6901)"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Implement JSON Pointer (RFC 6901) and export `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` from `src/index.ts`,
+as the first of a series of features that build on each other.
+
+## What was done
+
+- Added `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` and a `JsonPointerError` class to `src/index.ts`.
+- `resolvePointer` returns the whole document for the empty pointer, walks `/`-separated reference
+  tokens otherwise, decodes `~1` → `/` and `~0` → `~` in that order (per RFC 6901 §4), and recurses into
+  objects by member name and into arrays by a strict `0`/`[1-9][0-9]*` index.
+- It throws `JsonPointerError` for: a non-string pointer, a pointer that is non-empty and does not start
+  with `/`, a malformed `~` escape, a missing object member, an out-of-range or malformed array index
+  (including a leading zero or the `-` token), and stepping into a non-container value.
+- Wrote `src/index.test.ts` first (red/green): the RFC 6901 §5 worked example in full, nested
+  object/array traversal, and one test per error case above.
+- Added `typescript` and `@types/node` as devDependencies, and a `typecheck` script
+  (`tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit`), since the existing strict `tsconfig.json` had no tooling installed
+  to run it yet. Updated `tsconfig.json` with `allowImportingTsExtensions` and
+  `rewriteRelativeImportExtensions` so relative imports can use the `.ts` extension that Node's native
+  TypeScript execution requires (the `test` script runs `.test.ts` files directly, unbuilt).
+- Documented the new API in `README.md`.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm test`: 11/11 passing.
+- `npm run typecheck`: no errors.
